location flight_director_office : office study room
 short         the "FLIGHT director's office"
 south         corridor
 out           corridor

{look
print:
   Your office: small, not particularly tidy, four pale-green walls
   and a brown carpet worn smooth in a track between the door and
   the desk. The metal desk faces away from the window so the morning
   sun doesn't blind you when you read. The corridor is south.^
.
}

object desk : grey metal desk
 short         the "metal desk"
 static
 parent        flight_director_office

{examine
print:
   A grey metal desk, government-issue, scarred along its front edge
   by twenty years of belt buckles. On it: the open mission rules
   binder, a framed family photograph, a black rotary telephone, an
   ashtray with two stubbed-out cigarettes, and a coffee mug you
   forgot to take down to the auditorium.^
.
}

object photograph : photograph photo picture frame family wife daughter
 short         the "framed photograph"
 parent        flight_director_office

{examine
print:
   A small framed photograph: your six-year-old daughter on a red
   bicycle, your wife laughing at her with a hand on her own hair.
   Taken in your front yard last spring. You don't usually look at
   it during a launch. You don't usually need to.^
.
}

object binder : mission rules binder book manual
 short         the "mission rules binder"
 parent        flight_director_office

{examine
print:
   The mission rules binder, open at section 4.3 -- POWERED-FLIGHT
   ABORT MODES. The page is annotated in your handwriting, blue
   ballpoint, from the simulator runs three weeks ago. Most of your
   margin notes say things like ~don't trust GUIDANCE on this~ and
   ~ask EECOM~. Your past self is, on the whole, a good colleague.^
.
}

{read
print:
   You glance over section 4.3 again. The printed text is dense;
   your blue-ballpoint annotations are denser.^^
   ~MODE I (PAD ABORT - LET):~ pad to ten kilometres, launch
   escape tower. In the margin: ~tower jett at 90s -- check with
   RETRO before COMMIT~.^^
   ~MODE II (FULL ABORT):~ ten to forty-five kilometres. Reentry
   on the spacecraft's own systems, water landing in the Atlantic.
   ~don't trust GUIDANCE on this -- their numbers were off by
   four percent in the last sim run~.^^
   ~MODE III (CONTINGENCY ORBIT):~ forty-five kilometres up to
   second-stage cutoff. Coast to a lower orbit, then de-orbit. ~ask
   EECOM about consumables before committing to this -- the math
   depends on her reserves~.^^
   ~MODE IV (ABORT TO ORBIT):~ second-stage cutoff onward. You'd
   bring her back next pass.^
.
}

object calendar : calendar wall september
 short         the "wall calendar"
 static
 parent        flight_director_office

{examine
print:
   A wall calendar from the agency's PR office, today's date
   circled in red marker and labelled in your wife's handwriting:
   ~Be brilliant.~^
.
}

{read
print:
   The calendar page is September. Today's square is ringed in
   red marker; inside the ring, in your wife's quick handwriting:^^
   ~Be brilliant.~^^
   The week below carries her shorthand for the things you have
   promised to be home for after this -- ~F-day, 10am~ for the
   family-day picnic, ~Sarah recital Thurs~. Past her circles, the
   month rolls on without you, the way the months tend to.^
.
}

object window : window office parking lot
 short         the "window"
 static
 parent        flight_director_office

{examine
print:
   The window behind your chair gives onto the staff parking lot,
   half-empty in the September pre-dawn. A maintenance truck is
   crossing it, headlights painting brief stripes across the back
   of the next building.^
.
}

{look_through
print:
   You turn and look out. The staff parking lot is half-empty in
   the September pre-dawn -- a few stragglers' cars under the
   sodium lamps, the maintenance truck cutting its slow rounds.
   Beyond the lot, the cape proper is still dark, except for the
   floodlit pad, a small white pencil of light three kilometres
   away. From here you can't see the BURROUGHS, only the
   floodlight haze. Somewhere inside that haze a woman is sitting
   in a couch on top of seven thousand tonnes of cryogenics,
   waiting on you.^
.
}

