location viewing_gallery : viewing gallery observation deck visitors
 short         the "viewing gallery"
 west          corridor
 out           corridor

{look
print:
   The public viewing gallery: three rows of theatre-style seats
   raked down toward a glass wall that runs the length of the front
   of the room and looks down on the Mission Control floor below.
   Senators, reporters and engineers' wives speak to each other in
   the muted way people speak in a hospital chapel. The big board's
   rear glow flickers across all of them. The corridor is back to
   the west.^
.
}

object glass : glass wall window pane partition
 short         the "glass wall"
 static
 parent        viewing_gallery

{examine
print:
   A glass wall the length of the gallery, cantilevered out over
   the main Mission Control floor. From up here you can see all
   four banks of consoles, the back of every controller's head,
   and the big board glowing across the front wall. The audio is
   muted, mercifully, but the body language reads loud -- pencils
   tapping, eyes flicking, a hand combing nervously through hair.^
.
}

{look_through
print:
   You step up to the glass and look straight down at the floor.
   From here you can pick out the back of each controller's head
   and what their hands are doing. FIDO is leaning over her plot
   table, one finger tracing the predicted ascent arc. EECOM is
   hunched over his bus voltages, motionless. GUIDANCE is talking
   into his mic; you can see his throat working. RETRO is sitting
   absolutely still with his hands folded in front of his abort
   switches.^^
   The empty chair at the FLIGHT console is yours. They are too
   disciplined to keep looking at it. Some of them keep looking at
   it anyway.^
.
}

object seats : seats rows chairs theatre
 short         the "raked seating"
 static
 parent        viewing_gallery

{examine
print:
   Three rows of theatre-style seats, raked steeply down toward the
   glass. Senators, reporters, an astronaut's mother, two engineers'
   wives, and the Director of Mission Operations himself, who is
   sitting in the back row with his hands folded as a courtesy to
   the rest of the room.^
.
}

object vips : VIPs senators reporters wives guests visitors people audience
 short         the "VIPs"
 has           ANIMATE
 parent        viewing_gallery

{examine
print:
   The VIPs talk to each other in the kind of quiet voice you only
   use in a hospital chapel. None of them is looking at you.
   They are all looking past you, through the glass, at the four
   people on the floor below who are about to be more famous than
   they will ever be.^
.
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# +intro / dedication / scene -- delegate to the shared helper.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

{+intro
set player = kryten
execute "+setup_telemetry"
execute "+game_intro"
# Note: the live MET tick is *not* started here. Player has to type
# 'poll' to run the GO/NO-GO round-robin, which commits the count
# and starts the timer. This gives them time to learn the polling
# idiom (examine fido, eecom, ...) before the clock starts running.

# TEMP: smoke test the inbound-comms mechanism. Fires once, after the
# scene + voice/sound prompts have all resolved, so we can verify the
# Answer-call button, the tutorial hint, and the proxy dispatch into
# {ask_about_wind_fido}. Remove (along with smoke_test_fired) once a
# real event triggers a hail.
if intro_scene_shown = 1
   if pending_question_type = 0
      if smoke_test_fired = 0
         set smoke_test_fired = 1
         write "^FIDO leans across her console and keys her mic. The FIDO loop comms LED lights up.^"
         write "FIDO: ~Flight, FIDO. Wind balloon's back -- shear at altitude is up four knots from the briefing. Worth a look before the poll.~^"
         execute "+hail"<fido<wind
      endif
   endif
endif
}

# Pre-launch snapshot at T-1:00. Vehicle on the pad: motion values zero,
# pitch vertical, sitting on 1g, tanks topped off, comms locked, calm
# astronaut. Mass is the BURROUGHS stack fully fuelled.
{+setup_telemetry
set telemetry[TLM_MET]      = -60
set telemetry[TLM_ALTITUDE] = 0
set telemetry[TLM_HVEL]     = 0
set telemetry[TLM_VVEL]     = 0
set telemetry[TLM_RANGE]    = 0
set telemetry[TLM_FUEL]     = 99
set telemetry[TLM_THROTTLE] = 0
set telemetry[TLM_DELTAV]   = 9400
set telemetry[TLM_OXYGEN]   = 100
set telemetry[TLM_POWER]    = 100
set telemetry[TLM_CABIN]    = 101
set telemetry[TLM_SIGNAL]   = 100
set telemetry[TLM_PITCH]    = 90
set telemetry[TLM_GLOAD]    = 1
set telemetry[TLM_MACH]     = 0
set telemetry[TLM_LOXPSI]   = 268
set telemetry[TLM_MASS]     = 2940
set telemetry[TLM_HR]       = 64
set telemetry[TLM_AOA]      = 0
set telemetry[TLM_BUSV]     = 28
set telemetry[TLM_Q]            = 0   # dynamic pressure: zero on the pad
set telemetry[TLM_STAGE]        = 0   # S-IC, the first stage
set telemetry[TLM_ENGINES_LIT]  = 0   # no engines lit pre-ignition
set current_phase = PHASE_ASCENT
}

# Mark an inbound comms hail. The caller emits the narrative prose
# ("EECOM keys his mic...") first; this helper records who's on the
# loop and what they want to discuss, shows the one-time tutorial
# hint, and (in web mode) draws the Answer-call button.
#
# Usage:
#   write "EECOM keys his mic on the EECOM loop. His comms LED lights up.^"
#   execute "+hail"<eecom<leak
{+hail
set hailing_controller = arg[0]
set hailing_topic = arg[1]
set hailing_controller(keyed) = 1

# First hail in the game: nudge the player to learn the 'answer' verb.
# @ is +hail's own call_count -- the first call lands here regardless
# of which controller hails.
if @ = 1
   write "^(Type ~answer~ to take the call.)^"
endif
}

# Take whichever call is currently coming in. Proxies an 'ask
# <controller> about <topic>' command so the existing ask-about
# handlers fire normally, then clears the hail state.
# Write the bare callsign of a controller (e.g. "FIDO") with no
# trailing characters. Used by the keyed-status cues that need a
# short tag rather than the {The} macro's full "the FIDO controller".
{+write_callsign
if arg[0] = fido
   write "FIDO"
endif
if arg[0] = eecom
   write "EECOM"
endif
if arg[0] = guidance
   write "GUIDANCE"
endif
if arg[0] = retro
   write "RETRO"
endif
}

{+answer_call
if hailing_controller = 0
   write "No one is on the loop for you right now.^"
   set time = false
   return
endif

# 'answer' is itself the press-and-talk gesture: key the mic for the
# duration of the response so the player doesn't have to remember a
# separate 'press arm' mid-call. The mic stays open after the reply
# so a follow-up ask/tell flows naturally; the player can release it
# manually with another 'press arm'.
if mic_open = 0
   set mic_open = 1
   write "You press the push-to-talk bar; the loop LED lights green.^^"
endif

set hailing_controller(keyed) = 0
proxy "ask " hailing_controller{names} " about " hailing_topic{names}
set hailing_controller = 0
set hailing_topic = 0
}

# Override the standard library's +ask_about and +tell_about so that
# speaking on the loop requires the player's mic to be keyed. The game's
# definitions appear before the verbs.library #include at the bottom of
# this file, so they win first-match resolution and shadow the library
# versions. We reimplement the library's normal flow (can_talk gate,
# override lookup for per-topic handlers, generic fallback) so behaviour
# stays identical once the mic gate is satisfied.
{+ask_about
if mic_open = 0
   print:
      You start to form the question in your head and then catch
      yourself. The push-to-talk bar is flush; your mic is parked.
      Whatever you were going to ask stays inside the carrier hush of
      the headset. (~press arm~ to key your mic first.)^
   .
   set time = false
   return true
endif
if +can_talk<noun1 = true
   return true
endif
override
if noun2 = noun1
   write noun1{The}
   if noun1 has PLURAL
      write " are too shy to say much.^"
   else
      write " is too shy to say much.^"
   endif
   return true
endif
write noun1{The} " " noun1{doesnt} " have anything to say about " noun2{the}
write ".^"
}

{+tell_about
if mic_open = 0
   print:
      You almost speak and don't. The mic is parked; the loop carries
      only the steady hush of an open carrier. (~press arm~ to key
      your mic first.)^
   .
   set time = false
   return true
endif
if +can_talk<noun1 = true
   return true
endif
override
write noun1{The} " seem" noun1{s} " fascinated by your story about "
write noun2{the} .^
}

# Emit one line of the big-board status-light cluster. Anything inside
# the green band is GREEN; outside green but inside amber is AMBER;
# beyond amber is RED. arg slots:
#   string_arg[0]  label                   arg[3]  green band lo (inclusive)
#   arg[1]         current value           arg[4]  green band hi (inclusive)
#   string_arg[2]  unit suffix             arg[5]  amber band lo (inclusive)
#                                          arg[6]  amber band hi (inclusive)
{+status_light
write "   " string_arg[0] ": " arg[1] string_arg[2] "  ("
ifall arg[1] >= arg[3] arg[1] <= arg[4]
   write "GREEN"
else
   ifall arg[1] >= arg[5] arg[1] <= arg[6]
      write "AMBER"
   else
      write "RED"
   endif
endif
write ")^"
}

{+game_dedication
clear
style header
write "^ MISSION CONTROL^"
style normal
style note
write "Module 1 -- the launch.^"
style normal
write "by " game_author ", build " game_release "/" game_build "^^"
print:
   You are FLIGHT for this mission -- the flight director on console
   in Building 30, Houston. On the pad in Florida, fifteen hundred
   kilometres south-east, a four-stage liquid-fuelled vehicle stands
   ready: the BURROUGHS stack, fully fuelled and on internal power,
   with a single astronaut in the command capsule at the top.^^
   The count is at T-minus one minute. Your team is sharp and quiet.
   The decisions are yours; the consequences are seven thousand tonnes
   of cryogenic propellant and a woman strapped to the front of it.^^
.
}

{+game_scene
updatestatus
clear
style header
write "FINAL COUNT^"
style normal
write "T-00:01:00^^"
print:
   The Mission Control room is dim and warm and crowded. Four banks
   of monochrome monitors arc around you, each watched by an officer
   in a thin tie and shirtsleeves:^^
.
style bold
write "FIDO -- Flight Dynamics. "
style normal
print:
   Trajectory, range, weather. He watches where the vehicle is and
   where it ought to be.^^
.
style bold
write "EECOM -- Electrical, Environmental, Consumables. "
style normal
print:
   Power, oxygen, fuel pressures, crew biomedical -- everything that
   keeps the woman in the capsule alive.^^
.
style bold
write "GUIDANCE -- the on-board computer and inertial platform. "
style normal
print:
   The autopilot, the steering law, the maths.^^
.
style bold
write "RETRO -- abort options and range safety. "
style normal
print:
   The escape tower, the recovery footprint, every way for the
   mission to end short of orbit and still send the astronaut home.^^
   At the front of the room the big board shows the BURROUGHS stack
   on its pad, drawn in pale wireframe against the ochre Florida
   sky. Every status light shows green.^^
   Your headset crackles in your ear. A green LED above the FLIGHT
   loop selector -- your loop, since you ARE Flight -- on your
   console lights up at the same moment: the comms-active indicator
   that says someone has keyed their mic on that channel and is
   speaking to you right now. It is CAPCOM --
   the Capsule Communicator, the only person on the loop allowed to
   speak directly to the astronaut -- coming on for the first time
   today.^^
   "Flight, CAPCOM," he says, voice tinny and close. "Standing by
   for your GO/NO-GO poll."^^
   The poll he is asking for is a round-robin in which every
   controller in the room confirms ~GO~ for their console, one by
   one. Until it completes, the launch sequencer (the automatic
   system that takes the count through the final ten seconds to
   engine ignition) cannot commit. Initiating the poll is your job;
   CAPCOM will then relay your decisions down the loop to the
   spacecraft.^^
   The clock waits on you -- nothing is timed yet. Take a breath
   and type ~look~ to take stock of the room.^
.
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Status panel dispatcher. Each phase has its own bespoke layout in
# +update_status_<phase>; this function picks the right one. The 'cursor X Y'
# idiom works on both Glk (real TextGrid) and web (engine-emulated grid).
#
# Common geometry across all phases: 8 rows tall (rows 0 and 7 blank as
# top/bottom buffers, row 1 reserved for the ON LOOP indicator), 106
# cols wide, 4 cells per content row with cell starts at x=1, 29, 57,
# 85. Within each cell, label at offset 0, value at offset 10.
# Sub-acts can deviate but staying consistent helps the player's eye
# know where to look across phase transitions.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

{+update_status_window
if current_phase = PHASE_ASCENT
   execute "+update_status_ascent"
endif
# Future phases: add executes here as their +update_status_<phase>
# handlers are written, e.g.:
#   if current_phase = PHASE_DESCENT
#      execute "+update_status_descent"
#   endif
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ASCENT PANEL -- launch through MECO. The values that matter are the
# atmospheric-flight ones: dynamic pressure (Q) for max-Q, AoA, Mach,
# G-load, mass (drops as propellant burns), stage and engine count.
# Range to landing target / mission delta-V / oxygen reserve are not on
# this panel because they don't change meaningfully during ascent --
# they're back in the descent / coast / surface panels.
#
#   col 1 (FLIGHT command):  MET, Throttle, Pitch, G-load, Fuel
#   col 2 (trajectory):      Altitude, V-Vel, H-Vel, Mass, LOX press
#   col 3 (atmosphere):      Mach, Q, AoA, Stage, Engines
#   col 4 (crew/systems):    Crew HR (or DECIDE), Cabin, Signal, Power, Bus
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

{+update_status_ascent
# Row 0 is a top buffer between the title bar and the panel.

# Row 1: ON LOOP indicator. Used only when a controller has keyed
# their mic, so the player notices without having to examine
# anything. Left blank otherwise so there's a clear gap above the
# telemetry rows below.
if hailing_controller != 0
   cursor 1 1
   write "ON LOOP: "
   execute "+write_callsign"<hailing_controller
endif

# Row 2: MET / Altitude / Mach / Crew HR (or DECIDE during alarm).
cursor 1 2
write "MET"
cursor 11 2
if telemetry[TLM_MET] < 0
   set abs_met = 0
   set abs_met - telemetry[TLM_MET]
   write "T-" abs_met "s"
else
   write "T+" telemetry[TLM_MET] "s"
endif
cursor 29 2
write "Altitude"
cursor 39 2
write telemetry[TLM_ALTITUDE] " m"
cursor 57 2
write "Mach"
cursor 67 2
write telemetry[TLM_MACH]
cursor 85 2
if alarm_fired = true
   if decision_made = false
      write "DECIDE"
      set decision_seconds_left = decision_deadline
      set decision_seconds_left - telemetry[TLM_MET]
      if decision_seconds_left < 0
         set decision_seconds_left = 0
      endif
      cursor 95 2
      write decision_seconds_left "s"
   else
      write "Crew HR"
      cursor 95 2
      write telemetry[TLM_HR] " bpm"
   endif
else
   write "Crew HR"
   cursor 95 2
   write telemetry[TLM_HR] " bpm"
endif

# Row 3: Throttle / V-Vel / Q / Cabin.
cursor 1 3
write "Throttle"
cursor 11 3
write telemetry[TLM_THROTTLE] " %"
cursor 29 3
write "V-Vel"
cursor 39 3
write telemetry[TLM_VVEL] " m/s"
cursor 57 3
write "Q"
cursor 67 3
write telemetry[TLM_Q] " kPa"
cursor 85 3
write "Cabin"
cursor 95 3
write telemetry[TLM_CABIN] " kPa"

# Row 4: Pitch / H-Vel / AoA / Signal.
cursor 1 4
write "Pitch"
cursor 11 4
write telemetry[TLM_PITCH] " deg"
cursor 29 4
write "H-Vel"
cursor 39 4
write telemetry[TLM_HVEL] " m/s"
cursor 57 4
write "AoA"
cursor 67 4
write telemetry[TLM_AOA] " deg"
cursor 85 4
write "Signal"
cursor 95 4
write telemetry[TLM_SIGNAL] " %"

# Row 5: G-load / Mass / Stage / Power.
cursor 1 5
write "G-load"
cursor 11 5
write telemetry[TLM_GLOAD] " g"
cursor 29 5
write "Mass"
cursor 39 5
write telemetry[TLM_MASS] " t"
cursor 57 5
write "Stage"
cursor 67 5
if telemetry[TLM_STAGE] = 0
   write "S-IC"
else
   if telemetry[TLM_STAGE] = 1
      write "S-II"
   else
      if telemetry[TLM_STAGE] = 2
         write "S-IVB"
      else
         write "SC"
      endif
   endif
endif
cursor 85 5
write "Power"
cursor 95 5
write telemetry[TLM_POWER] " %"

# Row 6: Fuel / LOX press / Engines / Bus.
cursor 1 6
write "Fuel"
cursor 11 6
write telemetry[TLM_FUEL] " %"
cursor 29 6
write "LOX"
cursor 39 6
write telemetry[TLM_LOXPSI] " psi"
cursor 57 6
write "Engines"
cursor 67 6
write telemetry[TLM_ENGINES_LIT]
cursor 85 6
write "Bus"
cursor 95 6
write telemetry[TLM_BUSV] " V"

# Row 7 left blank as a bottom buffer before the maintext begins.
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Look text and examine handlers for each scenery object.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

{look
print:
   Building 30, second floor. Eight rows of consoles step down toward
   the front wall, where the big board glows with a wireframe of the
   BURROUGHS stack on its pad, every status light green. Your FLIGHT
   console is on the highest tier, central, with a clear sightline.^^
   To your left and right -- the four officers whose calls you weigh:
   FIDO (Flight Dynamics, watching trajectory and weather), EECOM
   (Electrical, Environmental and Consumables, watching power, oxygen,
   fuel and crew biomedical), GUIDANCE (the on-board computer and
   steering law) and RETRO (abort options and range safety).^^
   The room smells of cold coffee and warm transistors, and faintly
   of the ozone that rolls off the high-voltage tubes that have been
   running since dawn.^
.
if hint_examine_shown = false
   print:
      Try ~examine fido~ to key the loop on FIDO's console and hear
      her read.^
   .
endif
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# +local_header -- inject a Google Fonts <link> and a per-game CSS rule that
# uses Orbitron for the page title. webinterface.library calls this from
# inside <head> after its own styles, so the override wins.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

{+local_header
print:
   <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Orbitron:wght@500;700&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">
   <style>
   /* Taller header so the lunar-lander title art has room to breathe.
   The image's own aspect ratio (~3:1) means height 120px gives it
   ~360px of width on the left side of the bar. The rest of the
   header keeps the linkbar visible across full width. */
   #header { height: 120px; }
   img.titleimage { height: 120px; width: auto; display: block; }
   div.links { top: 90px; }
   #statuswin { top: 120px; font-family: 'Courier New', ui-monospace, monospace; font-size: 12pt; color: #ffffff; background-color: #2a2f36; }
   #statuswin:empty + #main { top: 120px; }
   #main { top: calc(120px + var(--status-lines) * 20px + 4px); }
   #header h1, #header h1 a { font-family: 'Orbitron', 'Courier New', monospace; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; }
   div.maintext { font-family: 'Orbitron', Georgia, serif; font-weight: 500; }
   /* Force Orbitron on every inline element inside maintext. The bare
   'b', 'p', 'a' rules in webinterface.css set Merriweather; without
   this they'd override the parent and break the sci-fi aesthetic. */
   div.maintext, div.maintext * { font-family: 'Orbitron', Georgia, serif; }
   div.maintext h1 { font-family: 'Orbitron', 'Courier New', monospace; color: #ffffff; text-align: center; padding-left: 0; letter-spacing: 0.08em; }
   div.maintext h2 { font-family: 'Orbitron', 'Courier New', monospace; letter-spacing: 0.06em; text-transform: uppercase; }
   /* Location titles: bold. Player commands: italic. */
   div.maintext b { font-weight: 700; color: inherit; }
   div.maintext i.jacl-command { font-style: italic; opacity: 0.8; }
   </style>
.
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ACT 1 PROTOTYPE -- live timer, alarm, GO/NO-GO, controller polling.
#
# The timer keeps firing during the decision prompt -- that's how the real
# countdown works. The player types commit / abort / hold (or c / a / h);
# while they think, MET advances and the cell-zero countdown ticks down.
# If MET reaches the deadline with no decision, +timer fires the timeout
# path and chooses the worst outcome for them.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Reference / accessibility verbs. 'controllers' (alias 'crew', 'staff',
# 'roster') prints the four-role summary; help / instructions are
# overridden below to point at it.
grammar controllers     >controllers
grammar crew            >controllers
grammar staff           >controllers
grammar roster          >controllers

grammar answer            >answer_call
grammar take call         >answer_call
grammar take the call     >answer_call
grammar accept call       >answer_call
grammar respond           >answer_call

# Pre-launch GO/NO-GO poll. 'poll' (or 'p') runs the round-robin and
# commits the count -- timer starts firing only after this. Decision
# verbs and single-letter shortcuts for the alarm window follow.
grammar poll       >poll
grammar p          >poll
grammar commit     >commit
grammar c          >commit
grammar abort      >abort
grammar a          >abort
grammar hold       >hold
grammar h          >hold

{+controllers
print:
   The four flight controllers on console with you in this room:^^
.
style bold
write "FIDO -- Flight Dynamics Officer. "
style normal
print:
   Watches the vehicle's trajectory: ground tracking radars on the
   pad, weather data up to ten kilometres altitude, and once she's
   flying, the instantaneous predicted impact point. Today, with
   the stack on the pad, FIDO is mostly reading wind.^^
.
style bold
write "EECOM -- Electrical, Environmental and Consumables. "
style normal
print:
   Power buses, fuel cells, cabin pressure, suit-loop oxygen, crew
   biomedical, and the ullage pressures of the four tanks. If the
   spacecraft has a leak, EECOM sees it first.^^
.
style bold
write "GUIDANCE -- Guidance, Navigation and Control. "
style normal
print:
   The on-board computer, the inertial platform (the gimballed gyros
   that tell the vehicle which way is up), and the steering law that
   flies the rocket. The autopilot is GUIDANCE's responsibility.^^
.
style bold
write "RETRO -- Retrofire and Range Safety. "
style normal
print:
   Watches every instant of flight and tells you, for THIS instant,
   what your abort options are: launch escape tower on the pad,
   first-stage shutdown during ascent, abort-to-orbit higher up.^^
   You are FLIGHT, the flight director, sitting central on the highest
   tier and weighing what each console tells you. CAPCOM, off to your
   left, is the only person on the loop who actually speaks to the
   astronaut.^^
   Useful queries: ~examine fido~ (or any controller) for that
   console's live read; ~ask [controller] about [topic]~ for more
   detail. Topics include ~role~, ~wind~, ~leak~, ~steering~ and
   ~tower~.^
.
}

# Override of verbs.library's +instructions (which 'help' maps to).
# Defining it here, before the libraries are #included at the bottom
# of the file, means JACL binds 'help' to this version instead.
{+instructions
print:
   MISSION CONTROL is an interactive fiction game in which you play
   FLIGHT, the flight director on console at NASA Mission Control
   in Houston during the launch of a fictional crewed lunar mission.
   The vehicle is the BURROUGHS stack, fully fuelled and on the pad
   in Florida; an astronaut sits in the command capsule at the top.
   Your job is to weigh the calls of four other officers around you
   and decide whether the count is COMMIT, HOLD or SCRUB.^^
   The game uses standard interactive fiction commands -- ~look~ to
   see your surroundings, ~examine [object]~ to look closer,
   ~north~ / ~south~ / ~east~ / ~west~ (or ~n~ / ~s~ / ~e~ / ~w~)
   to move between rooms. ~inventory~ (or ~i~) lists what you're
   carrying. ~save~ and ~restore~ work as you'd expect.^^
   Game-specific commands you'll need:^^
   * ~controllers~ (or ~crew~) -- a summary of the four officers
   in the room and what each console does. Read this first if
   the acronyms (FIDO, EECOM, GUIDANCE, RETRO, CAPCOM) aren't
   familiar.^
   * ~examine [controller]~ -- key the loop on that console and
   hear their current read. Reads change with the situation.^
   * ~ask [controller] about [topic]~ -- ask for their take on a
   specific subject. Topics: ~role~, ~wind~, ~leak~, ~steering~,
   ~tower~. Different controllers have different perspectives on
   the same topic.^
   * ~tell [controller] about [topic]~ -- relay information you
   have learned to a controller. They may revise their advice.^
   * ~poll~ -- run the GO/NO-GO round-robin and commit the count.
   The clock does not start ticking until you do.^
   * ~commit~, ~hold~, ~abort~ (or ~c~, ~h~, ~a~) -- decision
   verbs available when an alarm forces a call.^^
   The status panel above the text is your real-time telemetry.
   You don't need to memorise every value; the relevant ones are
   discussed in dialogue when they matter.^^
   Type ~controllers~ now if the acronyms are unfamiliar.^
.
}

{+poll
if timer_started = true
   print: "The count is already running, Flight."^.
   exit_function
endif
set hint_poll_shown = true
print:
   You key the FLIGHT loop. The room goes still. Around you, four
   officers settle into the round-robin you've drilled every working
   day for eight months. You speak the words flat and procedural:^^
   FLIGHT: "All controllers, GO/NO-GO for launch."^^
   FIDO, fast and clipped: "FIDO is GO."^
   EECOM, slow and unbothered: "EECOM is GO."^
   GUIDANCE, technical and a little formal: "GUIDANCE is GO."^
   RETRO, gravelly and short: "RETRO is GO."^^
   FLIGHT: "CAPCOM, we are GO for launch. Confirm the count."^^
   CAPCOM, on the loop and through the ground tracking station to
   the woman in the capsule: "Roger, Flight. Count is committed.
   T-minus sixty seconds. The clock is yours."^^
   The big board's countdown ticks live for the first time. You
   are out of preparation; everything from here is response.^
.
set timer_started = true
timer 1000
}

{+timer
# Silent tick: advance MET, repaint status. Nothing prints to maintext
# unless an event fires. Pre-launch the motion values stay zero -- the
# only thing actually moving is the clock.
set telemetry[TLM_MET] + 1

# At MET >= -30 (T-30s), FIDO calls wind shear and forces a GO/NO-GO.
if alarm_fired = false
   if telemetry[TLM_MET] >= -30
      set alarm_fired = true
      set decision_deadline = telemetry[TLM_MET]
      set decision_deadline + 24
      execute "+fire_alarm"
   endif
endif

# Window closes if no decision by deadline.
if alarm_fired = true
   if decision_made = false
      if telemetry[TLM_MET] >= decision_deadline
         set decision_made = true
         timer 0
         execute "+timeout_path"
      endif
   endif
endif

updatestatus
}

{+fire_alarm
print:
   The MET clock crosses T-30 and FIDO comes up sharp on the loop --
   sharp enough that across the room three other heads turn:^^
   FIDO, fast and clipped: "Flight, FIDO -- I have wind shear at ten
   kilometres altitude. Peak gust forty-six knots, against trajectory.
   That puts the vehicle through max-Q with twelve percent more lateral
   load than she's certified for. I am recommending HOLD. Recycle the
   count."^^
   GUIDANCE's chair slides forward. He cuts in over FIDO's last word:^^
   GUIDANCE, formal even with stress: "Flight, GUIDANCE -- the
   steering law has wind-shear margin. The autopilot pitches into the
   gust, trims angle of attack, we ride it. Recommend COMMIT."^^
   EECOM and RETRO are silent. The DECIDE counter on the panel has
   started ticking down -- twenty-four seconds before the launch
   sequencer locks itself out at T-6. Type ~commit~ (or ~c~), ~hold~
   (or ~h~), ~abort~ (or ~a~). Hitting the loop on a silent console
   (~examine eecom~, ~examine retro~) is free -- the clock keeps
   ticking either way.^
.
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Decision verbs. Each guards against being typed before the alarm or after
# a decision is logged, then routes to the appropriate branch.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

{+commit
if alarm_fired = false
   print: "There is nothing to commit to yet, Flight. Stand by."^.
   exit_function
endif
if decision_made = true
   print: "Decision logged, Flight. Stand by."^.
   exit_function
endif
if mic_open = 0
   print:
      You frame the word in your head and your thumb finds the bar --
      and stops. Your loop LED is dark. Nothing you say leaves your
      own jaw until you press it. (~press arm~ to key your mic.)^
   .
   set time = false
   exit_function
endif
set decision_made = true
timer 0
execute "+commit_path"
}

{+abort
if alarm_fired = false
   print: "Nothing to abort, Flight."^.
   exit_function
endif
if decision_made = true
   print: "Decision logged, Flight. Stand by."^.
   exit_function
endif
if mic_open = 0
   print:
      You start to call it and stop. The mic is parked; the call
      doesn't leave this seat. (~press arm~ to key your mic first.)^
   .
   set time = false
   exit_function
endif
set decision_made = true
timer 0
execute "+abort_path"
}

{+hold
if alarm_fired = false
   print: "Nothing to hold on, Flight."^.
   exit_function
endif
if decision_made = true
   print: "Decision logged, Flight. Stand by."^.
   exit_function
endif
if mic_open = 0
   print:
      You reach for the bar and stop yourself. With the mic parked,
      a HOLD call doesn't reach the controllers -- it just stays in
      your head. (~press arm~ to key your mic first.)^
   .
   set time = false
   exit_function
endif
set decision_made = true
timer 0
execute "+hold_path"
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Branch outcomes. Each is a stub for now; the real game would set up the
# next act here.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

{+commit_path
updatestatus
print:
   FLIGHT: "All controllers -- we are GO for launch. CAPCOM, pass
   the call."^^
   The room exhales. The count drops through T-6, T-3 -- five F-1
   analogues at the base of the BURROUGHS stack ignite. The building
   shakes a quarter-second later as the sound finally arrives from
   Florida. The vehicle clears the tower.^^
   At max-Q the autopilot trims as GUIDANCE promised. The structural
   margins protest as FIDO predicted. Strip charts go briefly, badly
   red, and then settle back to green. She is uphill -- with the
   trajectory degraded enough that you will spend the rest of the
   day repaying the delta-V you just spent on luck.^^
   ~[ACT 2 prototype ends here -- powered ascent and orbit insertion
   continue from this seat.]~^
.
}

{+abort_path
updatestatus
print:
   FLIGHT: "All controllers -- SCRUB the count. Safe the vehicle.
   CAPCOM, pass the wave-off."^^
   The launch sequencer halts at T-6. Hold-down arms grip. On the
   pad fifteen hundred kilometres south, the astronaut speaks one
   syllable into the loop you cannot quite make out, and the room
   briefly applauds her for it before the political reality of a
   one-day slip lands and the applause stops.^^
   ~[Scrub debrief ends here. Recycle for tomorrow's window.]~^
.
}

{+hold_path
updatestatus
print:
   FLIGHT: "FIDO, copy your HOLD. All controllers -- we recycle the
   count. CAPCOM, pass the hold."^^
   The launch sequencer freezes. Mile-long valves close in sequence,
   tank pressures equalise, and the wind data starts coming in fresh
   from the next balloon. Then, slow and clear and indifferent on
   the loop:^^
   EECOM: "Flight, EECOM. The LOX trend is still dropping. Two-six-
   six and falling. That is no longer gauge drift, Flight. That is
   a leak."^^
   The clock that is ticking now is the one EECOM warned you about.^^
   ~[Hold path ends here -- the leak diagnostic sub-act goes next.]~^
.
}

{+timeout_path
print:
   The DECIDE counter rolls through zero. The launch sequencer's own
   abort-on-no-call watchdog cuts the count at T-6 -- the worst version
   of a SCRUB, the one that is logged, written up, and remembered with
   your name attached. Every controller in the room is now wondering,
   privately and without saying so, who flies for them next time.^^
   ~[Timeout debrief ends here.]~^
.
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Standard libraries. webinterface.library / .css drive the web build;
# verbs.library / utils.library provide the parser and helpers used above.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

#include "webinterface.library"
#include "webinterface.css"
#include "verbs.library"
#include "utils.library"
